 By the triangle inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{x^2 + 400} + \sqrt{y^2 + 900} &\ge \sqrt{(x + 20)^2 + (y + 30)^2}.
\end{align*}
Equality occurs when $x = -20$ and $y = -30,$ but this is not in the given range of $x$ and $y.$

Now, we have
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{x^2 + 400} + \sqrt{y^2 + 900} + \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 - 80x - 100y + 4100} &\ge \sqrt{(x + 20)^2 + (y + 30)^2} + \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 - 80x - 100y + 4100}.
\end{align*}
By the triangle inequality again,
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{(x + 20)^2 + (y + 30)^2} + \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 - 80x - 100y + 4100} &\ge \sqrt{((x + 20) + x)^2 + ((y + 30) + y)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{(2x + 20)^2 + (2y + 30)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{4(x^2 + 10x + 100) + 4(y^2 + 15y + 225)} \\
&= \sqrt{4(x^2 + 400) + 4(y^2 + 900)} \\
&= 2\sqrt{x^2 + 400} + 2\sqrt{y^2 + 900}.
\end{align*}
Equality occurs when $x = -20$ and $y = -30,$ so the minimum value is $2\sqrt{(-20)^2 + 400} + 2\sqrt{(-30)^2 + 900} = 2 \cdot 40 + 2 \cdot 30 = 140.$
The answer is: 140.

