 To subtract numbers in base $9$, we can follow the same process as we do in base $10$, carrying over values when necessary.

The numbers given are $58_9$ and $18_9$. We subtract them as follows:

First, align the numbers vertically:
\[
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
& & 5 & 8 & _9 \\
- & & 1 & 8 & _9 \\
\cline{1-5}
& & & &
\end{array}
\]

Now, starting from the rightmost column, we attempt to subtract $8_9$ from $5_9$. However, this operation is not possible in base $9$ since $8 > 5$. To rectify this, we need to borrow $1$ from the next column to the left (the tens place). Borrowing $1$ from the tens place in base $9$ is equivalent to borrowing $9$ in base $10$, so we are effectively doing the operation $15_9 - 8_9$. Now we can perform the subtraction:

\[
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
& & 4 & (15)_9 & _9 \\
- & & & 8 & _9 \\
\cline{1-5}
& & 4 & & _9
\end{array}
\]

Next, we move to the tens column. Now we subtract $1_9 - 1_9$:

\[
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
& 4 & (15)_9 & _9 \\
- & 1 & 8 & _9 \\
\cline{1-5}
& 3 & 4 & _9
\end{array}
\]

So, $58_9 - 18_9 = 44_9$. The answer is: $44_9$.

 The greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points $A$ and $B$ occurs when the line is a diagonal line from the bottom left corner of the square to the top right corner of the other square, as shown below:

[asy]
unitsize(0.8 cm);

draw((0,0)--(2,2)--(2,3)--(4,3)--(4,2)--cycle);
draw((2,2)--(5,3));

