First, we find the solution modulo $77$ (the least common multiple of $7$ and $11$), using the method of successive substitution:
\begin{align*}
5 &\equiv 5 \pmod{7}, \\
5 + 11k &\equiv 10 \pmod{11} \implies 11k \equiv 5 \pmod{11} \implies k \equiv 3 \pmod{11}.
\end{align*}
So $5 + 11 \cdot 3 = 38 \equiv 10 \pmod{11}$. Therefore, $n \equiv 38 \pmod{77}$.

Next, we find the solution modulo $143$ (the least common multiple of $11$ and $13$), using the same method:
\begin{align*}
10 &\equiv 10 \pmod{11}, \\
10 + 7k &\equiv 10 \pmod{13} \implies 7k \equiv 0 \pmod{13} \implies k \equiv 0 \pmod{13}.
\end{align*}
So $10 + 7 \cdot 0 = 10 \equiv 10 \pmod{13}$. Therefore, $n \equiv 10 \pmod{143}$.

Now we have the system of congruences:
\begin{align*}
n &\equiv 38 \pmod{77}, \\
n &\equiv 10 \pmod{143}.
\end{align*}
To find the solution modulo $1001$, we can use the method of successive substitution again:
\begin{align*}
38 + 77k &\equiv 10 \pmod{143} \implies 77k \equiv -28 \pmod{143} \implies 77k \equiv 115 \pmod{143} \implies k \equiv 3 \pmod{143}.
\end{align*}
So $38 + 77 \cdot 3 = 269 \equiv 10 \pmod{143}$. Therefore, $n \equiv 269 \pmod{1001}$.

Since we are looking for the largest integer less than $2010$ that satisfies these congruences, we have $n = 2010 - 1001 = 1009$.
